From: "Peter C. McCluskey" <pcm@rahul.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Dec 1994 19:12:30 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-196] Singularity

 vincek@intergalact.com  (Vince Kerchner) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-12-125:
>> The book itself appears consistent with these possibilities, but things
>>Vinge has said elsewhere hint at something that is much harder to describe
>>to people of our technological level.
>
>Do you have any idea what that might be?  The other problem I have with the
>singularity is that it seems so ill-defined.  Kind of like 2010: "Something
>*wonderful* is going to happen..."  OK, what?

 Yes, it is ill-defined, and Arthur Clarke may well have provided the
original inspiration behind the concept.
 Predicting the nature of the singularity now is probably harder than
predicting today's computer usage in the 1950's, but I will take some
guesses.
 I don't expect anything that will look like an instantaneous change
from the viewpoint of the first uploads (other than disorientation -:),
or which will cause civilization to completely vanish to outside observers.
 I suspect the biggest qualitative changes will result from the ability to
improve the quality of one's thought processes. It will be possible to
experiment with aspect's of one's mind and improve on all those ugly
hacks that evolution has saddled us with.
 There will also be a big increase in the bandwidth and complexity of
communication between minds. Uploads are unlikely to use simulated speech
or simulated vision very much. I expect them to pass around data structures
that are more sophisticated than those used in today's software. This will
substantially increase the rate at which an upload can acquire new knowledge
relative to the upload's perceived time.
 And then there's nanotech. I'm hardly an expert on this subject, and don't
know whether it will happen before or after uploading, but the ability to
engineer sizeable structures at an atomic level will cause a fundamental
change in the way we handle matter. To a carbon-based human, nanotech might
just mean that a handfull of specialists controlled matter directly, but I
expect the improved communication between uploads to enable anyone to
acquire the knowledge needed to engineer almost anything just by thinking it.
